            <ArticleTitle>Coronary pressure never lies.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>248-56</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ccd.21528</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Fractional Flow Reserve (FFR), calculated by coronary pressure measurement, is the invasive gold standard to assess the hemodynamic significance of a coronary stenosis. FFR reliably indicates whether a stenosis is responsible for inducible ischemia and if percutaneous coronary intervention is appropriate. False positive or negative FFR is rare. Occasionally, however, a clinical or angiographic condition is encountered in which in first instance the FFR measurement contradicts the intuitive feeling of the interventionalist. Further examination in such cases, however, often reveals a clear physiologic explanation and in this manner FFR &quot;lifts the veil&quot;. Two such patients are presented in this case report and the reasons for apparent or actual false positive or negative FFR are discussed.</AbstractText>
